insert into public.questions (id, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
('q2-001',2,'2.1','mcq',1,$q$An attack group with massive funding maintains covert access to a defense contractor's network for 18 months, slowly exfiltrating design documents. Which threat actor is MOST likely responsible?$q$,$q$["Unskilled attacker","Hacktivist","Nation-state actor","Shadow IT"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Long dwell time, patience, espionage motive, and deep resources are the signature of nation-state/APT activity. Unskilled attackers use noisy public tools; hacktivists seek visibility for a cause; shadow IT is unsanctioned internal technology, not an attacker.$q$),
('q2-002',2,'2.1','mcq',1,$q$A group defaces a corporation's website with political slogans after the company announces a controversial policy. What is the attackers' MOST likely motivation?$q$,$q$["Financial gain","Philosophical/political beliefs","Espionage","War"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Defacement with slogans is classic hacktivism - ideology-driven statements. There is no monetization (financial), no covert collection (espionage), and no state conflict (war).$q$),
('q2-003',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$A database administrator who was passed over for promotion schedules a script to delete critical tables if her account is ever disabled. Which threat actor and attack does this describe?$q$,$q$["External actor deploying ransomware","Insider threat deploying a logic bomb","Hacktivist performing a DDoS","Nation-state deploying a rootkit"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A trusted employee weaponizing internal access with condition-triggered destruction is an insider threat using a logic bomb (triggers on the account-disable condition). Ransomware demands payment; DDoS floods services; rootkits hide access.$q$),
('q2-004',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$The marketing department signs up for an unapproved cloud file-sharing service to move large files. What does this represent?$q$,$q$["Insider threat (malicious)","Shadow IT","Supply chain attack","Hacktivism"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Unsanctioned technology adopted without IT approval is shadow IT - not malicious, but it creates unmanaged attack surface and data exposure. A malicious insider intends harm; the others are external attack patterns.$q$),
('q2-005',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$Which pair correctly distinguishes organized crime from an unskilled attacker?$q$,$q$["Organized crime: political motives; unskilled: financial motives","Organized crime: sophisticated tooling for profit; unskilled: existing public tools, low sophistication","Organized crime: internal access; unskilled: external only","Organized crime: disruption focus; unskilled: espionage focus"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Organized crime is financially motivated with real capability (ransomware operations, fraud infrastructure); unskilled attackers (script kiddies) run public tools they barely understand. Politics belongs to hacktivists/nation-states; internal access describes insiders.$q$),
('q2-006',2,'2.2','mcq',1,$q$Employees receive a text message claiming to be their bank, asking them to verify credentials via a link. What is this attack called?$q$,$q$["Vishing","Smishing","Whaling","Watering hole"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Phishing over SMS is smishing. Vishing uses voice calls; whaling is email phishing that targets executives; a watering hole compromises a website the victims frequent.$q$),
('q2-007',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$An attacker calls the help desk claiming to be a field technician who urgently needs a password reset for an executive account, citing a plausible ticket number. Which techniques are being combined?$q$,$q$["Pretexting and impersonation","Typosquatting and phishing","Watering hole and BEC","Disinformation and smishing"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The invented scenario (urgent ticket) is pretexting; posing as the technician is impersonation - a classic vishing combination. No look-alike domains (typosquatting), compromised websites (watering hole), or mass-influence campaigns (disinformation) are involved.$q$),
('q2-008',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$The CFO receives an email that appears to come from the CEO, urgently requesting a wire transfer to a new vendor account before end of day. What is this?$q$,$q$["Whaling targeting the CEO","Business email compromise","Watering hole attack","Typosquatting"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Impersonating an executive by email to redirect payments is business email compromise (BEC). Whaling would target the CFO with phishing to steal credentials; here the goal is fraudulent payment through impersonation. (These overlap, but BEC is the specific name for payment-fraud impersonation.)$q$),
('q2-009',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$Attackers compromise a regional industry association's website, knowing that employees of their true target visit it weekly. What attack is this?$q$,$q$["Supply chain attack","Watering hole attack","Typosquatting","Brand impersonation"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Compromising a site the target GROUP visits and letting them infect themselves is a watering hole attack. Supply chain attacks come through vendors/components; typosquatting uses look-alike domains; brand impersonation mimics a trusted brand's identity.$q$),
('q2-010',2,'2.2','mcq',1,$q$Users who mistype a popular retailer's URL land on a convincing fake site that harvests logins. What technique is the attacker using?$q$,$q$["DNS poisoning","Typosquatting","On-path attack","Session hijacking"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Registering misspelled look-alike domains to catch typos is typosquatting. DNS poisoning corrupts resolver caches (correct URL, wrong IP); on-path attacks intercept traffic; session hijacking steals established sessions.$q$),
('q2-011',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$Which attack vector does an air-gapped industrial network remain MOST exposed to?$q$,$q$["Phishing links in email","Removable media (USB drives)","Remote exploitation of open ports","Watering hole websites"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$With no network connectivity, the remaining bridge is physical: removable media carried in by staff/contractors (how Stuxnet spread). The other vectors all require network paths that an air gap removes.$q$),
('q2-012',2,'2.2','mcq',3,$q$A software vendor's build server is compromised, and a signed, malicious update is distributed to thousands of its customers. Which attack type is this, and why is it so effective?$q$,$q$["Watering hole - victims visit voluntarily","Supply chain attack - victims inherently trust vendor updates","Typosquatting - victims mistype the vendor URL","Insider threat - an employee planted malware"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Poisoning the vendor's software distribution is a supply chain attack (SolarWinds pattern): updates are signed and auto-trusted, bypassing customer defenses. Watering holes are compromised websites; the update channel, not typos or a customer-side insider, is the vector.$q$),
('q2-013',2,'2.3','mcq',2,$q$A vulnerability is being actively exploited in the wild, and the vendor has no patch available yet. What is this called?$q$,$q$["Legacy vulnerability","Zero-day","Race condition","Misconfiguration"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A flaw exploited before the vendor has a fix is a zero-day - defenders have had zero days to patch. Legacy vulnerabilities affect end-of-life systems; race conditions are timing flaws; misconfigurations are deployment errors.$q$),
('q2-014',2,'2.3','mcq',3,$q$A program verifies a file's permissions, then opens it a moment later. An attacker swaps the file for a symlink to /etc/shadow between those two steps. What vulnerability class is this?$q$,$q$["Buffer overflow","Time-of-check to time-of-use (TOCTOU)","Memory injection","Malicious update"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The gap between validation and use is a TOCTOU race condition - the resource changed after the check. Buffer overflows overwrite memory bounds; memory injection inserts code into processes; malicious updates poison software distribution.$q$),
('q2-015',2,'2.3','mcq',2,$q$An application crashes when a 500-character string is entered into a field expecting 50 characters, and researchers show attacker code execution is possible. What is the vulnerability?$q$,$q$["SQL injection","Buffer overflow","Cross-site scripting","Directory traversal"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Input exceeding a buffer's allocation that overwrites adjacent memory - potentially redirecting execution - is a buffer overflow. SQLi manipulates database queries; XSS injects browser script; traversal escapes directory roots.$q$),
('q2-016',2,'2.3','mcq',2,$q$A security review finds an S3-style storage bucket containing customer data is publicly readable. Which vulnerability category does this represent?$q$,$q$["Zero-day","Cloud misconfiguration","VM escape","Firmware vulnerability"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Publicly exposed storage is the classic cloud misconfiguration - the platform worked as configured; the CONFIGURATION was wrong. No unknown flaw (zero-day), hypervisor breakout (VM escape), or device code flaw (firmware) is involved.$q$),
('q2-017',2,'2.3','mcq',3,$q$An attacker breaks out of a guest virtual machine and executes code on the hypervisor, threatening every other VM on the host. What is this called?$q$,$q$["VM sprawl","Container escape","VM escape","Resource reuse"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Escaping guest isolation to reach the hypervisor is VM escape - the most severe virtualization vulnerability because it compromises all co-tenants. Sprawl is unmanaged VM proliferation; container escape targets a shared-kernel container host; resource reuse leaks data between tenants.$q$),
('q2-018',2,'2.3','mcq',1,$q$A user installs an app on their phone from an unofficial third-party website instead of the official app store. What is this practice called?$q$,$q$["Jailbreaking","Sideloading","Rooting","Bloatware"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Installing apps outside the official store is sideloading - it bypasses store vetting. Jailbreaking/rooting remove OS-level restrictions entirely; bloatware is unwanted preinstalled software.$q$),
('q2-019',2,'2.3','mcq',2,$q$Why do end-of-life operating systems pose a permanent security risk?$q$,$q$["They cannot run antivirus software","New vulnerabilities will never receive vendor patches","They are incompatible with firewalls","They automatically disable encryption"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$After end-of-life, the vendor stops issuing fixes, so every newly discovered flaw remains exploitable forever - only compensating controls (isolation, monitoring) or replacement reduce risk. The other statements are simply false.$q$),
('q2-020',2,'2.4','mcq',1,$q$Every file on a workstation is suddenly renamed with a strange extension, and a note demands cryptocurrency for the decryption key. What malware is this?$q$,$q$["Spyware","Worm","Ransomware","Rootkit"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Encrypting files and demanding payment is ransomware. Spyware covertly collects data; worms self-propagate; rootkits hide malware at the OS level - none announce themselves with ransom notes.$q$),
('q2-021',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$Malware spreads across the network automatically, infecting unpatched hosts with no user interaction whatsoever. Which malware type is this?$q$,$q$["Virus","Worm","Trojan","Logic bomb"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Self-propagation without user action defines a worm (WannaCry pattern). Viruses need a host file executed by a user; trojans trick users into running them; logic bombs wait for a trigger condition.$q$),
('q2-022',2,'2.4','mcq',1,$q$A user downloads a free PDF converter that works as advertised - but it also silently installs a backdoor. What malware type is the converter?$q$,$q$["Worm","Trojan","Rootkit","Keylogger"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Malware disguised as (or bundled inside) legitimate software that the USER willingly runs is a trojan. Worms spread themselves; rootkits are the hiding mechanism; a keylogger may be the payload, but the delivery disguise is the trojan.$q$),
('q2-023',2,'2.4','mcq',3,$q$An endpoint shows no suspicious files on disk, but memory analysis reveals malicious PowerShell running entirely in RAM, launched via WMI. What is this, and which control detects it BEST?$q$,$q$["Fileless malware; behavioral EDR","Rootkit; signature antivirus","Virus; disk scanning","Bloatware; application inventory"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Living-off-the-land malware that never writes to disk is fileless - signature/disk scanning finds nothing; behavior-based EDR watching process activity, script execution, and memory is the effective control. Rootkits hide components; viruses attach to files; bloatware is merely unwanted software.$q$),
('q2-024',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$After a compromise, antivirus scans report the system is clean, yet attackers retain access. Investigators later find kernel-level components hiding processes and files. What was installed?$q$,$q$["Spyware","Logic bomb","Rootkit","Adware"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Kernel-level concealment of processes/files that defeats scanning is a rootkit - its purpose is persistence through invisibility. Spyware collects data; logic bombs trigger on conditions; adware displays ads.$q$),
('q2-025',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$Which indicator MOST strongly suggests stolen credentials rather than a brute-force attempt?$q$,$q$["Thousands of failed logins on one account","A successful login from two continents forty minutes apart","An account lockout alert","CPU spikes on the mail server"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Impossible travel - successful logins from locations no human could traverse in the time - means someone else has valid credentials. Mass failures and lockouts indicate GUESSING that is failing; CPU spikes alone say nothing about authentication.$q$),
('q2-026',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$A DNS server is tricked into caching a false record so users typing the correct URL reach an attacker's server. What attack is this?$q$,$q$["Typosquatting","DNS poisoning","Domain hijacking","URL redirection via XSS"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Corrupting resolver caches with forged records is DNS (cache) poisoning - correct name, wrong destination. Typosquatting relies on user typos; domain hijacking steals registrar control of the domain itself; XSS operates in the victim's browser.$q$),
('q2-027',2,'2.4','mcq',3,$q$An attacker sends small spoofed DNS queries to open resolvers, setting the source address to the victim; each resolver sends a large response to the victim, overwhelming it. Which TWO techniques are combined?$q$,$q$["Reflection and amplification","Poisoning and tunneling","Hijacking and spoofing","Flooding and fragmentation"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Spoofing the victim's address so third parties respond to it is reflection; using a protocol whose responses vastly exceed the queries is amplification - together the classic reflected/amplified DDoS. Poisoning corrupts caches; tunneling hides data in DNS; the other pairs are not this pattern.$q$),
('q2-028',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$On a shared network, an attacker sends forged ARP replies so that traffic between a workstation and the gateway flows through the attacker's machine. What attack is underway?$q$,$q$["Replay attack","On-path (man-in-the-middle) attack","DNS tunneling","MAC flooding"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$ARP poisoning to insert yourself between two parties is an on-path attack - the attacker can read/modify traffic in transit. Replay re-sends captured data later; DNS tunneling exfiltrates via queries; MAC flooding overflows switch tables to force broadcast.$q$),
('q2-029',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$An attacker captures a user's session cookie and presents it to the web application to act as that user without ever knowing the password. What is this?$q$,$q$["Credential replay/session hijacking","Password spraying","Downgrade attack","Birthday attack"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Reusing captured authentication material (cookies, tokens, hashes) is replay/session hijacking - the password is unnecessary. Spraying guesses common passwords broadly; downgrade forces weak protocols; birthday attacks exploit hash collisions.$q$),
('q2-030',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$Security logs show login attempts using the password Spring2025! against 4,000 different accounts, a few attempts per account per day. What attack is this, and why is it shaped this way?$q$,$q$["Brute force - maximizing guesses per account","Password spraying - avoiding lockout thresholds","Credential stuffing - using breached pairs","Rainbow table - precomputed hashes"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$One common password tried across MANY accounts, slowly, is spraying - it stays under lockout and detection thresholds. Brute force hammers one account; credential stuffing replays breached username/password PAIRS; rainbow tables attack stolen hash databases offline.$q$),
('q2-031',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$Attackers use username/password pairs leaked from an unrelated website to log into your customer portal, succeeding wherever users reused passwords. What is this attack?$q$,$q$["Password spraying","Credential stuffing","Brute force","Keylogging"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Replaying breached credential PAIRS against other services is credential stuffing - it works because of reuse. Spraying uses one password against many accounts; brute force guesses systematically; keylogging captures keystrokes at the endpoint.$q$),
('q2-032',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$A web form field for a username is filled with: ' OR '1'='1' -- and the application logs the attacker in as the first user in the database. What flaw was exploited?$q$,$q$["Cross-site scripting","SQL injection","Directory traversal","Buffer overflow"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$SQL syntax in input altering the query's logic (making the WHERE clause always true) is SQL injection. XSS injects script into pages; traversal escapes the web root with ../ sequences; overflows corrupt memory.$q$),
('q2-033',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$An attacker posts a comment containing a script tag on a forum; every user who views the page has their session cookie sent to the attacker. Which attack variant is this?$q$,$q$["Reflected XSS","Stored XSS","CSRF","SQL injection"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Script saved server-side (in the comment) and served to all future visitors is STORED (persistent) XSS - the most dangerous variant. Reflected XSS requires each victim to click a crafted link; CSRF forges requests using the victim's existing session; SQLi targets the database.$q$),
('q2-034',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$A request for https://example.com/app?file=../../../etc/passwd returns the server's password file. What flaw does this demonstrate?$q$,$q$["SQL injection","Directory traversal","Privilege escalation","Race condition"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The ../ sequences walk out of the intended directory to arbitrary files - directory (path) traversal. Mitigate with input validation/canonicalization and least-privilege file access. No SQL, privilege change, or timing dependency is involved.$q$),
('q2-035',2,'2.4','mcq',3,$q$An attacker forces a client and server to negotiate TLS 1.0 with weak ciphers even though both support TLS 1.3, then breaks the session. What attack is this and its mitigation?$q$,$q$["Downgrade attack; disable legacy protocol versions","Birthday attack; longer hashes","Replay attack; session tokens","Collision attack; certificate pinning"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Forcing negotiation down to weaker protocols/ciphers is a downgrade attack; the fix is refusing legacy versions server-side (and TLS 1.3's downgrade protection). Birthday/collision attacks target hashes; replay reuses captured data.$q$),
('q2-036',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$Which password attack is performed entirely OFFLINE against a stolen database?$q$,$q$["Password spraying against the login portal","Rainbow table attack against unsalted hashes","Credential stuffing against the API","MFA fatigue push bombing"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Rainbow tables precompute hash lookups and run against a STOLEN hash database offline - no login attempts, no lockouts, invisible to the target. Spraying, stuffing, and push bombing all interact with live authentication systems.$q$),
('q2-037',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$A user reports their cursor moving on its own at night, and logs show remote sessions from an unknown IP using a legitimate remote-admin tool installed by a game mod. What is on this machine?$q$,$q$["A worm","A remote access trojan (RAT)","Adware","A logic bomb"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Covert remote control delivered by software the user installed voluntarily is a RAT. Worms spread autonomously; adware shows ads; logic bombs await trigger conditions - none give an attacker live interactive control.$q$),
('q2-038',2,'2.4','mcq',3,$q$SOC analysts notice DNS queries from one workstation: hundreds per minute, each to a unique long subdomain of the same external domain. What is MOST likely happening?$q$,$q$["Normal CDN behavior","DNS tunneling exfiltration","DNS cache poisoning","Reflected DDoS participation"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Data encoded into high-volume unique subdomain lookups of one domain is DNS tunneling - exfiltration through a protocol firewalls rarely block. CDNs use varied but recognizable domains at lower rates; poisoning targets resolvers; reflection would show spoofed-source floods, not unique subdomains.$q$),
('q2-039',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$Which of these is an ENVIRONMENTAL physical attack?$q$,$q$["Cloning an employee RFID badge","Cutting power and disabling HVAC to overheat a server room","Tailgating through the lobby","Shoulder surfing a password"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Attacking supporting infrastructure (power, cooling, water) is an environmental attack - systems fail without a single packet sent. Badge cloning is RFID cloning; tailgating and shoulder surfing are human/physical social techniques.$q$),
('q2-040',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$Logs show a normal user account suddenly creating new administrator accounts. Which attack stage does this indicate?$q$,$q$["Reconnaissance","Privilege escalation","Initial access","Denial of service"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A low-privilege account performing admin actions means the attacker elevated privileges (vertical escalation). Recon is information gathering; initial access already happened; DoS aims at availability, not account creation.$q$),
('q2-041',2,'2.5','mcq',1,$q$Which mitigation MOST directly limits how far an attacker can move after compromising one workstation?$q$,$q$["Network segmentation","Password complexity","Security awareness training","Data backups"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Segmentation constrains lateral movement - a compromised host can only reach its own zone. Password rules and training reduce initial compromise likelihood; backups aid recovery but do nothing to contain spread.$q$),
('q2-042',2,'2.5','mcq',2,$q$A firewall administrator adds a rule permitting only TCP 443 from the internet to the web server, with an implicit deny for everything else. What mitigation technique is this?$q$,$q$["Application allow list","Access control list","Isolation","Configuration enforcement"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Ordered permit/deny network rules are an ACL. Application allow lists control which EXECUTABLES run on hosts; isolation separates systems entirely; configuration enforcement corrects baseline drift.$q$),
('q2-043',2,'2.5','mcq',2,$q$After malware is found on a laptop, the security team moves it to a quarantine VLAN while investigating. Which mitigation concept is this?$q$,$q$["Patching","Decommissioning","Isolation (containment)","Least privilege"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Cutting a compromised host off from the network while preserving it for analysis is isolation/containment. Patching fixes vulnerabilities; decommissioning retires systems permanently; least privilege limits access rights in advance.$q$),
('q2-044',2,'2.5','mcq',2,$q$Which set of actions constitutes host hardening?$q$,$q$["Installing more applications for redundancy","Disabling unused services, closing unused ports, removing default credentials","Granting admin rights so users can self-patch","Enabling all logging and disabling the firewall"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Hardening MINIMIZES attack surface: fewer services, fewer open ports, no default credentials, plus patching, host firewall, and endpoint protection. Adding software, granting admin, or disabling the firewall each EXPANDS the attack surface.$q$),
('q2-045',2,'2.5','mcq',2,$q$An organization retires a file server: it is removed from the network, its drives are wiped and destroyed, its certificates revoked, and inventory updated. What is this process called?$q$,$q$["Containment","Decommissioning","Configuration enforcement","Patch management"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Formal end-of-life removal - network disconnection, data sanitization/destruction, credential/certificate revocation, inventory update - is decommissioning. Skipping it leaves forgotten, unpatched systems attackers love.$q$),
('q2-046',2,'2.5','mcq',2,$q$Which mitigation ensures a user in accounting cannot open engineering's source code repositories, even though both are on the same network?$q$,$q$["Encryption at rest","Least privilege / access permissions","Patching cadence","Monitoring"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Least privilege grants each identity only the access its role requires - accounting has no rights to engineering resources. Encryption protects stored data from theft, not from AUTHORIZED-looking access; patching and monitoring address different risks.$q$),
('q2-047',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$What distinguishes MISinformation from DISinformation?$q$,$q$["Misinformation targets executives; disinformation targets the public","Misinformation is false content shared without intent to deceive; disinformation is deliberately fabricated to deceive","Misinformation is verbal; disinformation is written","They are synonyms"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Intent is the difference: misinformation is false but spread believing it true; disinformation is deliberately created/spread to deceive (influence campaigns). Neither is defined by audience or medium.$q$),
('q2-048',2,'2.3','mcq',3,$q$A penetration tester finds the same cryptographic key hard-coded in every unit of a vendor's IoT camera firmware. Why is this a critical vulnerability?$q$,$q$["The key will expire simultaneously on all devices","Extracting the key from ONE device compromises EVERY device","Hard-coded keys prevent firmware updates","IoT devices cannot use cryptography efficiently"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A shared hard-coded secret means one extraction (from a purchased device) breaks the entire fleet - decrypting traffic or forging updates for every unit. Expiration, update capability, and crypto efficiency are not the core issue.$q$),
('q2-049',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$An organization's SIEM shows logging stopped entirely on a critical server for six hours overnight, then resumed. Why is this itself an indicator of compromise?$q$,$q$["Log storage was probably full","Attackers commonly disable or clear logging to hide their activity window","The server was in a maintenance window","Time synchronization failed"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Out-of-cycle or MISSING logs are a listed indicator - attackers disable logging while they work. Storage, maintenance, and NTP issues are possible benign explanations to rule out, but a silent gap on a critical system must be investigated as tampering.$q$),
('q2-050',2,'2.4','matching',3,$q$Match each attack to its PRIMARY target layer.$q$,$q${"left":["SQL injection","ARP poisoning","Stored XSS","Password spraying"],"right":["Database via application input","Local network traffic flow","Other users' browsers","Authentication systems"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$SQLi manipulates database queries through app input. ARP poisoning corrupts LAN address resolution to intercept traffic. Stored XSS executes in the browsers of users viewing the page. Spraying attacks login endpoints with common passwords across many accounts.$q$),
('q2-051',2,'2.1','matching',2,$q$Match the threat actor to their MOST typical motivation.$q$,$q${"left":["Nation-state","Organized crime","Hacktivist","Disgruntled insider"],"right":["Espionage","Financial gain","Ideological statement","Revenge"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Nation-states pursue intelligence/strategic advantage; criminal groups monetize (ransomware, fraud); hacktivists act on beliefs; wronged insiders act on revenge (or personal financial gain). Motivation shapes targeting, patience, and tooling.$q$),
('q2-052',2,'2.4','ordering',3,$q$Order these attacker actions into the typical attack progression.$q$,$q$["Phishing email delivers initial access","Malware establishes persistence","Attacker escalates privileges","Lateral movement to the database server","Data exfiltration"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$The standard chain: initial access (phishing) -> persistence (survive reboots) -> privilege escalation (gain admin) -> lateral movement (reach valuable systems) -> actions on objectives (exfiltration). Interrupting ANY earlier link prevents the later ones.$q$),
('q2-053',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$An employee finds a USB drive labeled Payroll Q3 in the parking lot and plugs it into their workstation. Which attack vector was used?$q$,$q$["Supply chain","Removable media baiting","Watering hole","Smishing"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Planting tempting infected media for curious victims is USB baiting - a physical/removable-media vector exploiting curiosity. Supply chain compromises vendors; watering holes compromise websites; smishing is SMS phishing.$q$),
('q2-054',2,'2.3','mcq',2,$q$Which describes a supply-chain SOFTWARE vulnerability rather than a hardware one?$q$,$q$["Counterfeit network switches with implants","A malicious npm package pulled into your application build","Interdicted servers modified in shipping","Cloned RFID access badges"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A poisoned third-party library entering through your build/dependency chain is the software supply-chain case. Counterfeit/interdicted equipment is hardware supply chain; badge cloning is a physical attack unrelated to supply chains.$q$),
('q2-055',2,'2.4','mcq',3,$q$Users complain the wireless network drops constantly; analysis shows a flood of spoofed 802.11 deauthentication frames. A second SSID identical to the corporate one has appeared. What is the attacker MOST likely attempting?$q$,$q$["Bluesnarfing","Forcing clients onto an evil twin AP to intercept credentials","RFID cloning","DNS tunneling"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Deauth floods knock clients off the real AP; the look-alike SSID (evil twin) catches them reconnecting, putting the attacker on-path for credential capture. Bluesnarfing is Bluetooth data theft; RFID cloning copies badges; DNS tunneling is an exfiltration channel.$q$);
